## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical SRE skills: incident triage, region-aware rollouts, backpressure, circuit breaking, and rollback discipline beyond basic incident response.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-region latency analysis
- Cache stampede mitigation
- Per-region deployment strategies (canary, rollouts)
- Safe rollback criteria and instrumentation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple circuit breaker (pseudo)
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(threshold) { this.open = false; this.failures = 0; this.threshold = threshold; }
  onFailure() { this.failures++; if (this.failures > this.threshold) this.open = true; }
  allow() { return !this.open; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to a service mesh with traffic shifting?
- How would you verify rollback correctness across all regions post-incident?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to handle external dependency fragility, multi-tenant isolation, and production risk management. The question evaluates end-to-end instrumentation, resilience patterns, and practical operational procedures rather than theoretical knowledge.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end observability with OpenTelemetry
- Retry budgets with exponential backoff and jitter
- Circuit breakers with configurable thresholds and recovery
- Backpressure through token bucket rate limiting
- Idempotent retries with deduplication keys
- Progressive canary rollouts with automated rollback
- Incident runbooks and alerting thresholds
- SLO/SLI definition and percentile-based monitoring

## Code Example

```javascript
// Circuit breaker around identity provider
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(threshold = 5, timeout = 3000) {
    this.failureCount = 0;
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.timeout = timeout;
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
    this.lastFailure = null;
  }

  async execute(operation) {
    if (this.state === 'OPEN') {
      if (Date.now() - this.lastFailure > this.timeout) {
        this.state = 'HALF_OPEN';
      } else {
        throw new Error('Circuit breaker is OPEN');
      }
    }

    try {
      const result = await operation();
      this.onSuccess();
      return result;
    } catch (error) {
      this.onFailure();
      throw error;
    }
  }

  onSuccess() {
    this.failureCount = 0;
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
  }

  onFailure() {
    this.failureCount++;
    this.lastFailure = Date.now();
    if (this.failureCount >= this.threshold) {
      this.state = 'OPEN';
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes hands-on ability to design and automate incident response with real-world constraints (Kubernetes, Kafka, Istio). It tests metric selection, safe auto-remediation, and rollback discipline.

## Key Concepts
- Metrics: p95 latency, error rate, Kafka lag, cache metrics
- Auto-remediation: rate limiting, circuit breakers, HPA, Kafka backpressure
- Feature flags and canaries for safe rollback
- Validation window and explicit rollback criteria
- Safety and regional considerations in a live prod system

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple heuristic for rollback decision
function needsRollback(baseline, current) {
  const latencySpike = current.p95Latency > baseline.p95Latency * 1.2;
  const errorSpike = current.errorRate > baseline.errorRate * 1.5;
  const lagExcess = current.kafkaLag > baseline.kafkaLag * 1.4;
  return latencySpike || errorSpike || lagExcess;
}
```

